//? Title: Estimate crossed and nested random effects
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Crossed and nested group structure

In a crossed design, several participants can respond to the same
item. A response can then share a participant effect with one set of
rows and an item effect with another. In a nested design, each plot
belongs to one site. A plot label that repeats at another site
identifies a different plot.

```{cpp, id=crossed-and-nested, file="figures/crossed_nested_groups.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::graphics;
    const DiagramNodeStyle upper{.width=2.65, .height=.8, .font_size=18, .padding=9,
                                .fill={219,234,254}, .border={37,99,235}};
    const DiagramNodeStyle lower{.width=2.45, .height=.8, .font_size=18, .padding=9,
                                .fill={254,243,199}, .border={180,83,9}};
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    Diagram(49, 980)
        .label({1.6, 3.2}, "Crossed", 23)
        .label({11.5, 3.2}, "Nested", 23)
        .node("a", {0,2}, "Person A", upper).node("b", {3.2,2}, "Person B", upper)
        .node("i", {0,0}, "Item 1", lower).node("j", {3.2,0}, "Item 2", lower)
        .link("a", "i").link("a", "j").link("b", "i").link("b", "j")
        .node("s1", {8.5,2}, "Site A", upper).node("s2", {14.5,2}, "Site B", upper)
        .node("a1", {7,0}, "A / Plot 1", lower).node("a2", {10,0}, "A / Plot 2", lower)
        .node("b1", {13,0}, "B / Plot 1", lower).node("b2", {16,0}, "B / Plot 2", lower)
        .edge("s1", "a1").edge("s1", "a2").edge("s2", "b1").edge("s2", "b2")
        .save_svg("build/crossed_nested_groups.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/crossed_nested_groups.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Crossed items can be shared across participants. Each nested plot has a single parent site.)
\alttext(Both people are linked to both items in the crossed design. In the nested design, each site points to its own two plots.)

> Fit a variance for each random-effect block

The two fits use the same fixed design and each has two random
intercepts. The crossed fit indexes the second block by item. The
nested fit indexes it by the site and plot together. The solver
estimates a variance for each block and a residual variance by REML.

```{cpp, id=multiple-variance-blocks, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/variance_components.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge;
    const double first_effect[]{-1.1, .2, -.6, 1.5};
    const double item_effect[]{.8, -.7, -.1};
    const double plot_effect[]{.3,-.6,.4, .2,.8,-.7, -.9,.4,.3, .7,-.4,-.2};
    std::println("| Design | First block variance | Second block variance | Residual variance |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const bool nested : {false,true}) {
        linalg::DenseMatrix<double> fixed(24, 2, 1);
        linalg::DenseMatrix<double> first(24, 4), second(24, nested ? 12 : 3);
        std::vector<double> y;
        for (int a = 0; a < 4; ++a)
            for (int b = 0; b < 3; ++b)
                for (const double t : {-1.,1.}) {
                    const auto row = y.size();
                    fixed(row, 1) = t;
                    first(row, a) = 1;
                    second(row, nested ? 3*a + b : b) = 1;
                    const auto effect = nested ? plot_effect[3*a + b] : item_effect[b];
                    y.push_back(2 + .7*t + first_effect[a] + effect + .24*std::cos(3*a + 2*b + t));
                }
        const auto fit = stats::fit_variance_components(fixed, y, {first,second});
        if (fit.status != stats::VarianceComponentsStatus::Converged)
            throw std::runtime_error("Expected an interior variance-component fit");
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", nested ? "Nested" : "Crossed",
                     fit.variances[0], fit.variances[1], fit.residual_variance);
    }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Design | First block variance | Second block variance | Residual variance |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Crossed | 1.283 | 0.572 | 0.039 |
| Nested | 1.218 | 0.357 | 0.041 |
// result_end

For conditional predictions, supply new rows of the random designs
in the same block and column order. An all-zero row gives a new group
the population-average random effect.

[Bates et al., Fitting Linear Mixed-Effects Models Using lme4|https://doi.org/10.18637/jss.v067.i01]
